WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.930 --> 00:00:12.530
It’s a pretty sweet gig that we’ve got...to
act as a tour guide through the microcosmos,

00:00:12.530 --> 00:00:17.430
and we’ve been fortunate to be joined by
you fellow travelers, bringing all of your

00:00:17.430 --> 00:00:24.420
insightful thoughts, and very funny quips
and also good questions in the comments section.

00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:30.980
So for today, we wanted to turn it around
and let some of the most frequent questions

00:00:30.980 --> 00:00:37.600
we’ve received guide this episode so we
can talk more about how things work behind

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:41.760
the scenes here, and how it impacts this
journey.

00:00:44.020 --> 00:00:49.420
Many of you have asked how we find and care
for our microscopic friends.

00:00:49.420 --> 00:00:57.000
Well, James, our collector of microbes and
master of microscopes, takes samples literally

00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:04.360
from everywhere he can, but his favorite spot
is a park just next to his house.

00:01:04.360 --> 00:01:09.520
Collecting these samples requires some high
tech equipment like...a selfie stick.

00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:15.110
Which James bought and converted into a portable
sampling stick that lets him reach tough spots.

00:01:15.110 --> 00:01:16.200
It’s lightweight.

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:21.750
It’s collapsable, what else do you need!

00:01:21.750 --> 00:01:26.700
Once we have collected our specimens, we can’t
ignore them, we have to take care of them,

00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:29.500
keep them alive and thriving.

00:01:29.500 --> 00:01:34.990
For James, that means a few things: protecting
them from environmental factors like direct

00:01:34.990 --> 00:01:40.680
sunlight or sudden changes in temperature,
while also providing them with a nutritious

00:01:40.680 --> 00:01:43.040
setting that allows them to grow.

00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:45.979
This of course looks different for different
microbes.

00:01:45.979 --> 00:01:51.680
For algae, James adds a couple drops of liquid
plant fertilizer into the water and gives

00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:53.690
them some natural sunlight.

00:01:53.690 --> 00:02:00.290
But heterotrophs like ciliates and flagellates,
they need food, and James has a number of

00:02:00.290 --> 00:02:04.979
different homemade solutions that provide
that sustinance.

00:02:04.979 --> 00:02:11.920
From nearly the beginning of microbe hunting,
a favorite food has been a hay infusion, and

00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:14.419
that is something that James uses as well.

00:02:14.419 --> 00:02:19.090
It's basically just...boiled hay.

00:02:19.090 --> 00:02:23.730
Other times, he might sprinkle some cooked
rice grains into the sample, or add drops

00:02:23.730 --> 00:02:24.730
of milk.

00:02:24.730 --> 00:02:30.140
It’s a whole menu designed to suit different
organisms, but no matter what they demand,

00:02:30.140 --> 00:02:37.319
the goal is the same: to make sure we’re
taking care of our little pond buddies.

00:02:37.319 --> 00:02:42.900
Now to the most frequent question of all,
our equipment.

00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:47.749
When it comes time to get a closer look at
our cultures, we’ve used two different microscopes

00:02:47.749 --> 00:02:50.790
to film the footage you’ve seen on this
channel.

00:02:50.790 --> 00:02:56.840
The first one is a microscope that James cobbled
together from various parts for just under

00:02:56.840 --> 00:03:02.499
$200, creating hundreds of hours of recorded
footage that we still use.

00:03:02.499 --> 00:03:05.620
So this one is a microcosmos original.

00:03:05.620 --> 00:03:10.049
You can’t buy it, there is only one, and
there is no brand name.

00:03:10.049 --> 00:03:15.730
After we launched our channel, James got a
sponsorship from a microscope company—maybe

00:03:15.730 --> 00:03:21.309
the most niche (and, we think, one of the
coolest) sponsorships you can get—and since

00:03:21.309 --> 00:03:29.239
then, he’s used a Motic BA310 microscope
and a Fujifilm X-T3 camera.

00:03:29.239 --> 00:03:35.180
With this microscope, we’ve been able to
expand on the kind of footage we can show

00:03:35.180 --> 00:03:40.319
thanks to the different types of light we
can use, like these paramecia that we observed

00:03:40.319 --> 00:03:42.169
under polarized light.

00:03:42.169 --> 00:03:47.260
We’re very excited about all the different
forms of light you can use with this microscope

00:03:47.260 --> 00:03:54.809
and what they reveal about the microcosmos,
and you will see more of that in future episodes.

00:03:54.809 --> 00:04:01.699
Another common question...how do we prepare
and observe our samples...how do we actually

00:04:01.699 --> 00:04:04.489
get a look at these organisms?

00:04:04.489 --> 00:04:08.159
Well, the procedure is fairly similar each
time.

00:04:08.159 --> 00:04:14.069
We first take a drop from our samples and
place that on a glass slide, and then we cover

00:04:14.069 --> 00:04:20.260
that drop with another super-thin piece of
glass called a coverslip.

00:04:20.260 --> 00:04:28.240
That creates an extremely thin film of water
between those two pieces of glass, and that

00:04:28.240 --> 00:04:32.319
is what we observe when we make our videos.

00:04:32.319 --> 00:04:38.000
Some of you have asked why we can see microbes
moving left, right, up, and down, but they

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:41.880
never seem to move towards or away from us.

00:04:41.880 --> 00:04:47.169
Of course, microbes live in a three dimensional
universe, but our footage does not always

00:04:47.169 --> 00:04:51.080
reflect that because of how microscopes work.

00:04:51.080 --> 00:04:57.409
The high magnification objectives that we
use have a low depth of field, meaning the

00:04:57.409 --> 00:05:01.410
area that is in focus is a very thin slice.

00:05:01.410 --> 00:05:10.080
So we force the microcosmos into a two dimensional
space, this very thin area between the two

00:05:10.080 --> 00:05:11.659
pieces of glass.

00:05:11.659 --> 00:05:17.930
Now sometimes we actually make that area thicker
for larger organisms by putting some stuff

00:05:17.930 --> 00:05:18.990
under the coverslip.

00:05:18.990 --> 00:05:23.840
Maybe it’s some decaying plant matter or
some tiny grains of sand.

00:05:23.840 --> 00:05:29.000
In those cases, however, it becomes easier
for those organisms to wander out of focus

00:05:32.980 --> 00:05:39.900
Our microscope also shapes the way we observe
the color of these organisms, or often, what

00:05:39.900 --> 00:05:41.729
seems to be the lack thereof.

00:05:41.729 --> 00:05:48.199
We’ve been asked why so many of them are
transparent, and, well, transparency comes

00:05:48.199 --> 00:05:49.930
down to two things.

00:05:49.930 --> 00:05:57.090
First, how much light there is, and second
how much stuff there is blocking that light.

00:05:57.090 --> 00:06:04.360
In the case of microscopic observation...there’s
a lot of light, and the bodies are tiny.

00:06:04.360 --> 00:06:08.860
Some of these organisms just lack the pigments
that would make them colorful.

00:06:08.860 --> 00:06:15.349
But for others, the light source in the microscope
is just too strong, overpowering their pigments

00:06:15.349 --> 00:06:18.530
so they appear, to our eyes, transparent.

00:06:18.530 --> 00:06:23.719
And you would be transparent too if you were
only a few microns thick.

00:06:23.719 --> 00:06:29.130
Some of you also want to know whether we use
stains to create our footage.

00:06:29.130 --> 00:06:35.240
Stains are a common laboratory and medical
technique that uses dyes to bind to different

00:06:35.240 --> 00:06:40.979
parts of cells and thus help visualize their
components and processes.

00:06:40.979 --> 00:06:47.650
While they are often beautiful and very informative,
stains can also be harmful to cells, potentially

00:06:47.650 --> 00:06:52.020
even killing them so we have chosen to keep
the microorganisms un-stained.

00:06:53.480 --> 00:06:58.139
There have also been questions about what
our magnification notes mean.

00:06:58.139 --> 00:07:02.819
Those 100x’s and 200x’s you see up in
the corner.

00:07:02.819 --> 00:07:10.400
Those numbers refer specifically to the magnification
created by the microscope, not how much larger

00:07:10.400 --> 00:07:15.889
the image is as it appears on your screen,
relative to its actual size.

00:07:15.889 --> 00:07:21.639
Now that is a subtle difference, but subtle
differences can be very important.

00:07:21.639 --> 00:07:27.749
After all, some of you are watching this on
your phones, others are watching on a computer

00:07:27.749 --> 00:07:33.759
screen, others might be watching on a big
70” flat screen on your wall and if you

00:07:33.759 --> 00:07:36.300
are, that’s awesome.

00:07:36.300 --> 00:07:37.300
Thank you.

00:07:37.300 --> 00:07:42.129
We, however, do not know what size device
you’re watching this on, but you’re seeing

00:07:42.129 --> 00:07:49.949
the same 100x in the corner because that describes
the optics of the microscope, not the size

00:07:49.949 --> 00:07:53.669
of the screen you’re using to watch YouTube
videos.

00:07:53.669 --> 00:07:58.639
To fix this, we’ve gotten a lot of comments
asking us to use a scale bar instead of the

00:07:58.639 --> 00:08:04.990
magnification, and we’ve been thinking about
non-intrusive ways of implementing that.

00:08:04.990 --> 00:08:09.139
And finally, we’ve had questions about how
real-time our footage is.

00:08:09.139 --> 00:08:15.580
We always make sure to note whether the footage
we’re showing has been sped up.

00:08:15.580 --> 00:08:21.460
Sometimes the microbes we show you are super
chill and relaxed, and sometimes, they are

00:08:21.460 --> 00:08:27.539
very much not chill, moving faster than many
of us might expect.

00:08:27.539 --> 00:08:32.419
We’re asked sometimes whether any particular
piece of footage has been sped up, and rest

00:08:32.420 --> 00:08:39.910
assured that unless we’ve noted it, that
very active microbe is just very active.

00:08:40.760 --> 00:08:42.680
I mean, calm down friend.

00:08:42.680 --> 00:08:44.740
You can take a breather.

00:08:44.740 --> 00:08:47.829
And so can all of us every once in a while.

00:08:47.829 --> 00:08:52.940
Thank you so much for all of your questions
and all your comments over these months.

00:08:52.940 --> 00:08:59.030
We hope that by answering them, you find new
things to ponder and ask us about the micrococosmos.

00:08:59.030 --> 00:09:04.660
A question that doesn’t pose new questions
is no question at all.

00:09:06.880 --> 00:09:11.410
And if, say, you are wondering whether this
channel reminds us of the cell stage in the

00:09:11.410 --> 00:09:16.980
game Spore…the answer is of course yes,
yes it very much does.

00:09:18.300 --> 00:09:24.000
Thank you for coming on this journey with
us as we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

00:09:24.000 --> 00:09:30.320
We also just recently launched our very first piece of microcosmos merch!

00:09:30.320 --> 00:09:33.800
I'm wearing it right now, though you cannot see that,

00:09:33.800 --> 00:09:36.120
because there are no cameras on me,

00:09:36.120 --> 00:09:37.550
but I promise I am.

00:09:37.550 --> 00:09:39.960
It's a Stentor Coeruleus pin,

00:09:39.960 --> 00:09:47.380
and the image of the Stentor on the pin is roughly 40x the size of what a real-world Stentor coerules is,

00:09:47.380 --> 00:09:50.080
So when people see it, you can tell them that.

00:09:50.080 --> 00:09:54.880
And you can own this little piece
of the microcosmos, there's a link, in the description.

00:09:54.880 --> 00:10:00.680
Thank you so much to all of our patrons on
Patreon who make it possible for us to do

00:10:00.680 --> 00:10:03.779
this weird thing that we love doing

00:10:03.779 --> 00:10:07.880
And if you want to see more from our Master
of Microscopes James, you can check out Jam

00:10:07.880 --> 00:10:09.410
and Germs on Instagram.

00:10:09.410 --> 00:10:14.911
And, of course, if you want to subscribe to
Journey to the Microcosmos, I bet you can

00:10:14.920 --> 00:10:17.920
figure out how to do that.

